        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title" id="disc-spec-explorer">Spec Explorer</h3>
        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Spec Explorer is the specification structure and navigation layer. Use it to understand how the specification is organized, browse sections and headings, see which artifacts belong to each section, identify healthy versus unhealthy sections, and navigate into Traceability.</p>
        <p><strong>How to access:</strong> Run analysis on <a href="/extract" class="ug-inline-link">Specification Review</a>, then click the <strong>Spec Explorer</strong> tab in the Analysis Results area. The explorer builds automatically from the analysed spec — no re-upload required.</p>
        <p><strong>When to use:</strong></p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li>Navigating a large or complex specification by structure rather than by extracted item</li>
            <li>Seeing section health, including requirements, tests, clarifications, coverage percentage, and explicit Covered, Partial, or Missing labels</li>
            <li>Understanding User Story ownership without expanding every section</li>
            <li>Opening a requirement or section and jumping into Traceability when coverage needs deeper analysis</li>
            <li>Using Map View to understand Heading to Requirements to Tests to Success Criteria structure</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Role clarity:</strong> Traceability &amp; Coverage is for coverage analysis. Flow View is for QA readiness. Spec Explorer is for specification structure and navigation. Extraction Review is for extraction quality review. Architecture View is for technical assumptions and architecture.
        </div>
        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Note:</strong> Spec Explorer is not a standalone menu item. It only appears after a successful Specification Review analysis. Refreshing the page will restore the Spec Explorer from the saved session automatically.
        </div>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title" id="disc-flow-view">Flow View</h3>
        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> A QA Readiness dashboard for User Stories. Flow View groups requirements, acceptance tests, success criteria, and implementation tasks by user story — and surfaces gaps, health status, and readiness scores without requiring manual inspection of each story. It answers: <em>What is ready for testing? What is missing? What should be fixed next?</em></p>
        <p><strong>How to access:</strong> Run analysis on <a href="/extract" class="ug-inline-link">Specification Review</a>, then click the <strong>Flow View</strong> tab in the Analysis Results area.</p>
        <p><strong>When to use:</strong></p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li>Getting a business-level QA readiness picture before starting testing</li>
            <li>Identifying which user stories are missing tests, success criteria, or task coverage</li>
            <li>Prioritising QA work based on health status and story priority (P1 first)</li>
            <li>Understanding user journeys and business workflows captured in the spec</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Story Health Badges</h4>
        <p>Each user story shows a health badge in its header — visible without expanding the story:</p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>✓ Covered</strong> — all requirements have tests and at least one success criterion is linked.</li>
            <li><strong>⚠ Missing Tests</strong> — one or more requirements have no linked acceptance tests.</li>
            <li><strong>⚠ Missing Success Criteria</strong> — all requirements are covered by tests, but no success criteria are defined.</li>
            <li><strong>⚠ Partial Coverage</strong> — some requirements are covered, others are not.</li>
            <li><strong>⚠ Missing User Story Mapping</strong> — requirements exist but are not assigned to any user story.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">QA Readiness Score</h4>
        <p>Every story shows a QA Readiness percentage based on two dimensions:</p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Test coverage (50%)</strong> — the proportion of requirements that have at least one linked acceptance test.</li>
            <li><strong>Success Criteria presence (50%)</strong> — whether at least one success criterion is linked to the story.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A score of 100% means all requirements are covered and success criteria are present. The breakdown panel shows requirements covered / total, success criteria count, total tests, and task status.</p>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">QA Work Queue</h4>
        <p>At the top of Flow View, the <strong>What needs attention?</strong> work queue surfaces the highest-priority gaps immediately. Each item is clickable and filters the story list to show only the affected stories:</p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li>Stories missing tests</li>
            <li>Stories missing success criteria</li>
            <li>Requirements missing tests (gap count)</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Flow View Filters</h4>
        <p>Quick filter chips let you focus on specific gap types without scrolling through all stories:</p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>All</strong> — all user stories.</li>
            <li><strong>Covered</strong> — stories with full test coverage and success criteria.</li>
            <li><strong>Missing Tests</strong> — stories where at least one requirement has no linked tests.</li>
            <li><strong>Missing Success Criteria</strong> — stories with test coverage but no success criteria.</li>
            <li><strong>Ready For Testing</strong> — stories that meet the covered threshold.</li>
            <li><strong>High Priority (P1)</strong> — stories tagged as P1 in the specification.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Priority Sorting</h4>
        <p>Stories are sorted by priority — P1 first, then P2, then P3, then unspecified. Within the same priority level, document order is preserved. Priority is extracted from labels like <code>(P1)</code>, <code>(P2)</code>, <code>(P3)</code> in the story heading.</p>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Missing Success Criteria Warning</h4>
        <p>When a story has no linked success criteria, a prominent <strong>⚠ Missing Success Criteria</strong> badge appears in the story header (visible when collapsed) and inside the Success Criteria step. Success Criteria define measurable outcomes and are required for a story to reach 100% QA Readiness.</p>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Task Coverage Status</h4>
        <p>The Implementation Tasks step distinguishes between: <strong>Tasks not imported</strong> (no tasks.md has been uploaded) and tasks linked to requirements (shown when task import is available). Use Task Explorer or Import tasks.md to add task coverage.</p>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title" id="disc-extraction-review">Extraction Review</h3>
        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Validate and improve extraction quality. Extraction Review is an advanced, optional quality-control workspace for accepting, rejecting, diagnosing, and linking AI-extracted artifacts.</p>
        <p><strong>How to access:</strong> Run analysis on <a href="/extract" class="ug-inline-link">Specification Review</a>, then click the <strong>Extraction Review</strong> tab in the Analysis Results area.</p>
        <p><strong>When to use:</strong> Most testers will start in Traceability &amp; Coverage and may never need Extraction Review. Use it when extraction quality needs verification, when artifacts need manual correction, or when traceability relationships need adjustment.</p>
        <div class="ug-table-scroll">
            <table class="ug-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Review Status</th>
                        <th>Meaning</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>AutoAccepted</strong></td>
                        <td>Automatically extracted and included.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Manually Accepted</strong></td>
                        <td>Reviewed and confirmed.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Needs Review</strong></td>
                        <td>Potential issue requiring attention.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Rejected</strong></td>
                        <td>Excluded from Traceability calculations.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p><strong>Link Related Candidates:</strong> Use the right-side panel to connect requirements to tests, connect requirements to success criteria, improve traceability coverage, and correct extraction relationships. These changes affect Traceability &amp; Coverage calculations.</p>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title" id="disc-traceability-view">Traceability &amp; Coverage (Analysis Tab)</h3>
        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Shows the QA risk picture first: coverage status, missing tests, orphan tests, missing success criteria, missing user story mappings, and relationship gaps. This is the <em>default</em> view shown after analysis because it answers what is covered, what is missing, and what should be fixed next.</p>
        <p><strong>How to access:</strong> Run analysis on <a href="/extract" class="ug-inline-link">Specification Review</a>. The Traceability &amp; Coverage tab is active by default in Analysis Results.</p>
        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Note:</strong> Use Traceability &amp; Coverage from inside Specification Review. It shows relationships <em>within the current analysis session</em> and is the primary tester workspace for validation coverage.
        </div>
        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Traceability &amp; Coverage vs. QA Artifact Library:</strong> Coverage analysis belongs in Traceability &amp; Coverage — it is the single source of truth for coverage metrics, gap analysis, missing tests, orphan tests, and requirement relationships. The <a href="/scenarios" class="ug-inline-link">QA Artifact Library</a> is a separate repository for storing, discovering, and reusing curated QA assets — it does not calculate or display coverage metrics. Publishing to the library is optional; Traceability &amp; Coverage works without it.
        </div>
        <p><strong>When to use:</strong></p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li>Identifying requirements that have no test candidates in the current analysis</li>
            <li>Finding tests that are not linked to any requirement</li>
            <li>Reviewing unresolved clarification items before accepting candidates</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Understanding Traceability</h4>
        <p>Traceability connects business intent, requirements, validation, and implementation evidence. It helps answer practical review questions:</p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li>Which requirements are covered by tests?</li>
            <li>Which user stories are fully validated?</li>
            <li>Which requirements are missing coverage?</li>
            <li>Which tests are not linked to requirements?</li>
            <li>Which implementation tasks support a requirement?</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Traceability Model</h4>
        <p>The model is hierarchical. User Stories describe the business need. Requirements describe specific system behavior. Success Criteria describe measurable outcomes. Tests verify requirements. Tasks describe the implementation work that supports those requirements.</p>
        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Traceability hierarchy:</strong>
            Business Goal &darr; User Story &darr; Requirement (FR) &darr; Success Criteria &darr; Acceptance Tests &darr; Implementation Tasks
        </div>
        <div class="ug-table-scroll">
            <table class="ug-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Layer</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>User Story</strong></td>
                        <td>Describes a user or business need.</td>
                        <td>US1 - Search for Children</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Requirement</strong></td>
                        <td>Defines specific system behavior that can be covered.</td>
                        <td>FR-001 Search by Name / National ID / DUF / BirkID</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Success Criteria</strong></td>
                        <td>Defines measurable outcomes that the requirement should satisfy.</td>
                        <td>SC-001 search usability, SC-003 security filtering</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Acceptance Test</strong></td>
                        <td>Verifies that the requirement behaves as expected.</td>
                        <td>Test-001, Test-002, Test-003</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Implementation Task</strong></td>
                        <td>Shows the delivery work that implements or supports the requirement.</td>
                        <td>Task-014, Task-015</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <pre class="ug-code-block"><code>US1 - Search for Children
|- FR-001 Search by Name / National ID / DUF / BirkID
|  |- SC-001
|  |- Test-001
|  |- Test-002
|  `- Task-014
|
`- FR-002 Security Filtering
   |- SC-003
   |- Test-003
   `- Task-015</code></pre>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Why Coverage Is Requirement-Centric</h4>
        <p>Coverage is calculated at requirement level. A User Story may contain many requirements, and each requirement can have a different coverage status. Traceability &amp; Coverage answers "which requirements are covered?" directly — selecting a requirement shows its User Story, linked tests, Success Criteria, coverage status, and coverage reason in a single view.</p>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Traceability &amp; Coverage Tabs</h4>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Requirements:</strong> The primary view. Select any coverage requirement to inspect its User Story, Success Criteria, linked tests, Tasks, coverage status, and coverage reason. Search and filter the list to focus on specific areas.</li>
            <li><strong>Gaps:</strong> Actionable diagnostics for Missing User Story, Missing Success Criteria, Missing Tests, and Orphan Tests. Clicking a health dashboard card navigates here automatically.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Coverage Calculation</h4>
        <p>Coverage is calculated from coverage-eligible requirements only. By default, that means explicit Functional Requirements such as FR-001, FR-002, and FR-025. A requirement is covered when it has at least one confirmed or deterministic linked test, or when it is explicitly marked as covered by a deterministic traceability rule.</p>
        <p><strong>Coverage Requirements</strong> are the requirements included in coverage calculations. Extracted clarifications, decisions, assumptions, architecture notes, metadata, bullet details, and other supporting artifacts are not counted as coverage requirements.</p>
        <div class="ug-table-scroll">
            <table class="ug-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Requirement</th>
                        <th>Tests</th>
                        <th>Success Criteria</th>
                        <th>Status</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>FR-001</strong></td>
                        <td>3</td>
                        <td>1</td>
                        <td>Covered</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>FR-025</strong></td>
                        <td>0</td>
                        <td>0</td>
                        <td>Missing Tests</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">What Does Not Contribute To Coverage</h4>
        <p>Supporting artifacts provide important context, but they are not requirements and do not require tests. They appear in the Artifacts tab and do not affect the coverage denominator or missing-test gap count.</p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li>Clarifications</li>
            <li>Decisions</li>
            <li>Assumptions</li>
            <li>Architecture Notes</li>
            <li>Metadata</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">User Story Coverage</h4>
        <p>User Story coverage is derived from the coverage status of the requirements inside that story. For example, if US1 contains FR-001 Covered, FR-002 Covered, and FR-003 Missing Tests, then US1 is partially covered.</p>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Success Criteria Coverage</h4>
        <p>Success Criteria help determine whether requirements satisfy business outcomes. They provide an additional validation layer between Requirements and Tests, so reviewers can see not only whether a requirement has tests, but whether those tests support the intended outcome.</p>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Gap Categories</h4>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Missing User Story:</strong> A coverage requirement is not mapped to a User Story, so business ownership is unclear.</li>
            <li><strong>Missing Success Criteria:</strong> A coverage requirement has no linked measurable outcome.</li>
            <li><strong>Missing Tests:</strong> A coverage requirement has no linked acceptance tests.</li>
            <li><strong>Orphan Tests:</strong> A test exists but is not linked to any coverage requirement.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Navigating with Summary Cards</h4>
        <p>The six <strong>summary cards</strong> at the top of Traceability &amp; Coverage are clickable and navigate directly to the relevant view. Click any card to jump there:</p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Requirements</strong> — opens the Requirements tab showing all coverage requirements.</li>
            <li><strong>Covered</strong> — opens the Requirements tab (search or scroll to see covered items).</li>
            <li><strong>Missing Tests</strong> — opens the Gaps tab focused on requirements without tests.</li>
            <li><strong>Missing User Stories</strong> — opens the Gaps tab focused on requirements with no user story mapping.</li>
            <li><strong>Missing Success Criteria</strong> — opens the Gaps tab focused on requirements with no success criterion.</li>
            <li><strong>Orphan Tests</strong> — opens the Gaps tab focused on tests not linked to any requirement.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>When navigating from a card to the Gaps tab, only the relevant gap group is shown. Use <strong>Show all gaps</strong> to return to the full list.</p>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Gaps Tab as QA Work Queue</h4>
        <p>The <strong>Gaps</strong> tab organises outstanding traceability gaps into a prioritised work queue. Each gap group is collapsible and shows the count and affected items:</p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Requirements without Tests</strong> — the most critical gap. Each item here is a requirement at risk of being unvalidated at release.</li>
            <li><strong>Missing User Story</strong> — requirements without a business owner. Fixing these improves traceability and prioritisation clarity.</li>
            <li><strong>Missing Success Criteria</strong> — requirements without a measurable outcome definition. Fixing these strengthens acceptance criteria.</li>
            <li><strong>Orphaned Tests</strong> — tests not linked to any requirement. These may be redundant or may cover undocumented behaviour.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Clicking an item in any gap group navigates to the Requirements tab with that requirement selected, showing its full details.</p>

        <h4 class="ug-feature-title">Recommended Review Order</h4>
        <ol class="ug-fd-list">
            <li><strong>Matrix:</strong> Understand overall requirement coverage.</li>
            <li><strong>Gaps:</strong> Identify missing coverage — use it as a QA work queue.</li>
            <li><strong>Requirements:</strong> Investigate specific requirements with full details.</li>
            <li><strong>Artifacts:</strong> Review assumptions, decisions, and clarifications.</li>
            <li><strong>Graph:</strong> Understand the relationships across user stories, requirements, tests, success criteria, and tasks.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title" id="disc-architecture-view">Architecture View</h3>
        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Organises extracted candidates around technical concerns and architectural boundaries. Architecture View groups items by technical area (Security, API, Integration, Domain, etc.) — helping architects and senior developers assess the systemic scope of the specification.</p>
        <p><strong>How to access:</strong> Run analysis on <a href="/extract" class="ug-inline-link">Specification Review</a>, then click the <strong>Architecture View</strong> tab in the Analysis Results area.</p>
        <p><strong>When to use:</strong></p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li>Reviewing a specification from an architecture or systems perspective</li>
            <li>Identifying cross-cutting concerns and integration points captured in the spec</li>
            <li>Assessing technical risk areas before implementation planning</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="ug-subsection-title" id="disc-task-explorer">Task Explorer</h3>
        <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Task Explorer is the <strong>Implementation Coverage</strong> workspace. It answers whether the specification is being implemented: which requirements have tasks, which requirements are missing implementation, which success criteria lack task coverage, and where delivery risk exists.</p>
        <p><strong>How to access:</strong> Upload both spec.md and tasks.md on <a href="/task-alignment" class="ug-inline-link">Implementation Review</a> and run analysis. Then click the <strong>Task Explorer</strong> tab in the results area. The explorer builds automatically.</p>
        <p><strong>When to use:</strong></p>
        <ul class="ug-fd-list">
            <li>Checking which user stories, functional requirements, success criteria, tests, and architecture notes have implementation tasks</li>
            <li>Finding requirements, success criteria, security work, or testing work with missing implementation links</li>
            <li>Using the Impact tab as a delivery-risk queue for missing implementation work</li>
            <li>Drilling into individual tasks to see linked user stories, requirements, success criteria, test assets, architecture notes, implementation status, and coverage impact</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Traceability &amp; Coverage = Validation Coverage.</strong> Use it to answer whether requirements are tested and validated.</p>
        <p><strong>Task Explorer = Implementation Coverage.</strong> Use it to answer whether requirements are implemented and whether delivery gaps remain.</p>
        <div class="ug-callout ug-callout-tip">
            <strong>Note:</strong> Task Explorer is not a standalone menu item. It only appears after a successful Implementation Review analysis. The explorer can also be used standalone by pasting tasks.md content directly.
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 7: AI-Assisted Development ──────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="ai-dev" class="ug-section">
        <h2 class="ug-section-title">AI-Assisted Development</h2>
        <p class="ug-section-lead">QA Review Studio is built for teams using AI to accelerate implementation. The platform helps you maintain quality, traceability, and governance even when velocity is high.</p>

        <div class="ug-ai-flow-row">
            <div class="ug-ai-flow">
                <div class="ug-ai-node">
                    <div class="ug-ai-node-label">Specification</div>
                    <div class="ug-ai-node-desc">Requirements, user stories, acceptance criteria</div>
